By running the AI locally on your MacBook Air, your robot becomes completely self-contained. It will process voice commands instantly, require absolutely zero internet connection, and you will never see a quota or rate-limit error again.

In this, we are using Terminal to download Ai by the  command "ollama run llama3.2"

How Ollama Works on your Mac
The App: When you opened the Ollama app from your Applications folder, it didn't open a window. Instead, it started a silent background server and put a little Llama icon in your Mac's top menu bar (near your clock and Wi-Fi icon). As long as that icon is there, your local AI server is online.

The Terminal Command: When you typed ollama run llama3.2, your Mac reached out to the internet, downloaded the "Brain" (the neural network weights), and loaded it into your Mac's memory.

The Connection: Your master_robot.py file is now programmed to send messages to http://localhost:11434. That is the exact secret backdoor port that the Ollama background server is listening to.

Your Final Launch Sequence
Since Ollama is running and the model is downloaded, you are completely done with the setup. You just need to turn on your robot.

Open a fresh terminal and run your standard boot sequence:

Bash
# 1. Go to your project folder
cd "Desktop/My Project"

# 2. Open your Python toolbox
source venv/bin/activate

# 3. Start the Master Server
python3 master_robot.py
Open your browser to http://localhost:5004, click the microphone on your Cyberpunk dashboard, and give your robot a command. Your Mac's processor will do all the thinking, and the arm will move!
